{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["And the reason for <b>\"If he said thus, speckled shall be your wages\"</b> is that at first he gave him two markings — speckled and spotted — as well as brown among the sheep, then he changed his mind to give a different marking, and he would change it every year, and the flocks would give birth accordingly. And this was not through the rods, for he is recounting to them the great deed that HaShem did with him, to make wonders year after year, as he said, \"and Elohim did not permit him to harm me.\" It is also possible that he would change his wages after the flocks were already impregnated, and they would give birth according to Jacob's desire, as seemed right in the eyes of the Fashioner to do. And in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 74:3): The Holy One, blessed be He, foresaw what Laban was destined to do to Jacob our father, and He forms a likeness like that which issues from it — \"if he said thus\" is not written here, but rather \"if he said thus,\""], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "The quotation from Bereshit Rabbah 74:3 contrasts \"אָמַר\" (he said) with \"יֹאמַר\" (he will say); Ramban's point turns on the difference in verbal aspect, which is preserved by rendering both forms literally despite the near-identical English."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:21:52.816774848Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}